WEBVTT

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:03.730
Every green strategy you already know has

00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:05.060
a line in a spreadsheet.

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:08.170
Here are the three numbers that decide

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:09.620
whether your building gets built.

00:00:10.770 --> 00:00:13.580
Before anyone talks about rent, the

00:00:13.630 --> 00:00:15.470
building is a pile of costs.

00:00:16.210 --> 00:00:18.500
Underwriters sort that pile into five

00:00:18.550 --> 00:00:22.470
buckets. Land, hard costs, soft costs,

00:00:22.970 --> 00:00:25.210
the developer's fee, and contingency.

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:29.290
$38 .5 million for 120 units.

00:00:30.610 --> 00:00:35.360
$320 ,500 each. Land, foundation, and

00:00:35.410 --> 00:00:38.060
roof are fixed. Everything in between

00:00:38.110 --> 00:00:40.230
scales with each unit you add.

00:00:40.850 --> 00:00:42.050
Hold that thought.

00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:44.630
Two kinds of money build it.

00:00:45.770 --> 00:00:47.900
The bank lends at six and a quarter

00:00:47.950 --> 00:00:51.220
percent and wants the same payment every

00:00:51.270 --> 00:00:52.690
month for 30 years.

00:00:53.310 --> 00:00:54.930
Full building or not.

00:00:56.050 --> 00:00:59.570
Investors want 15 % and get paid last,

00:01:00.110 --> 00:01:01.510
only from what's left.

00:01:02.330 --> 00:01:05.100
The bank doesn't lend 70 % because it's

00:01:05.150 --> 00:01:07.880
generous. It lends what the income can

00:01:07.930 --> 00:01:11.530
safely repay. So we need the income.

00:01:12.650 --> 00:01:15.530
Rent, minus vacancy, minus expenses,

00:01:16.030 --> 00:01:17.690
equals net operating income.

00:01:18.690 --> 00:01:21.960
The building's paycheck, $2 .23 million a

00:01:22.010 --> 00:01:24.450
year. Every line is a design decision.

00:01:24.930 --> 00:01:27.140
The envelope sets the utility line, the

00:01:27.190 --> 00:01:28.700
construction type sets the insurance

00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:31.250
line, the amenities set the vacancy line.

00:01:31.970 --> 00:01:34.220
Divide the paycheck by the cost, and this

00:01:34.270 --> 00:01:36.970
building yields 5 .79%.

00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:40.560
The loan costs 7 .39 % a year.

00:01:40.610 --> 00:01:43.330
That gap is the whole story.

00:01:44.450 --> 00:01:46.840
How many times over does the paycheck

00:01:46.890 --> 00:01:48.370
cover the loan payment?

00:01:49.330 --> 00:01:51.370
The bank wants 1 .25.

00:01:52.210 --> 00:01:56.010
This building covers it 1 .12 times.

00:01:56.790 --> 00:01:58.630
Barely, and not enough.

00:01:59.290 --> 00:02:01.480
So the bank shrinks the loan until

00:02:01.530 --> 00:02:03.870
coverage reaches 1 .25.

00:02:04.810 --> 00:02:08.550
The building needs 26 .9 million.

00:02:09.150 --> 00:02:11.600
The bank offers 24 .1.

00:02:11.650 --> 00:02:16.660
A $2 .79 million hole opens up that

00:02:16.710 --> 00:02:18.870
someone has to fill with expensive money.

00:02:19.930 --> 00:02:22.340
After the bank is paid, what's left

00:02:22.390 --> 00:02:23.590
belongs to the investors.

00:02:24.370 --> 00:02:27.880
2 .1 % on the 11 and a half million they

00:02:27.930 --> 00:02:31.000
put in. Over 15 years, with rent growth

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:33.340
and a sale, that becomes a 10 and a half

00:02:33.390 --> 00:02:35.570
percent return. They wanted 15.

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:39.180
Nothing about this building is wrong.

00:02:39.980 --> 00:02:42.250
It dies in underwriting, not in design

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:45.350
review, and every move that fixes it is

00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:47.560
something you already know how to draw.
